import GroupApproximation.Manuscript.NonMF.AcylindricallyHyperbolic

/-!
# Hull's small cancellation theorem, as a hypothesis

`non_mf_groups_exist.tex` cites four results in the section *"A torsion-free
group with full MF radical"* that it does not prove.  This module states them —
each exactly in the form the manuscript uses it — and bundles all four as the
fields of a single structure `HullInputs`.

Nothing here is proved and nothing here is postulated.  `HullInputs` is a
hypothesis: a consumer that wants the manuscript's conclusions takes a term of
it as an argument, so every theorem downstream carries the four citations
visibly in its own statement.  `Saturation.saturation` is the consumer, and
`lem:saturation` reads there with `(hHull : HullInputs)` as its leading
hypothesis.

## The four cited results

* `HullInputs.smallCancellation` — `thm:hull`, **Hull, Theorem 7.1**, in the
  finite-set formulation the manuscript records.  The manuscript's preamble
  explains the reduction: *"Given a finite set `F ⊆ G`, choose a ball in
  `Γ(G,A)` containing `F` and apply Hull's injectivity-radius formulation.  This
  gives the finite-set version recorded below."*  Only the finite-set version is
  stated, because only it is used.
* `HullInputs.kerNormallyGenerated` — the manuscript's separate remark,
  *"inspection of Hull's construction, applied successively to `t₁, …, t_m`,
  shows that `ker q` is normally generated by `m` elements."*  It is a distinct
  citation, not a consequence of the statement of `thm:hull`, and it is what
  makes the saturation quotient finitely presented.
* `HullInputs.osinNonElementary` — **Osin, Lemma 7.1**, used by the proof of
  `lem:saturation` in the form *"`N ∩ gNg⁻¹` infinite for every `g` implies `N`
  acts non-elementarily on `Γ(G,A)`"*.  The hypothesis is exactly the
  repository's `HullSuitable.IsSNormal`, whose docstring already records that
  its geometric half is this lemma of Osin's.
* `HullInputs.twoGeneratedSuitable` — **Hull, Corollary 5.7 and Lemma 5.8**,
  used in the form *"`N` contains two elements `h₁, h₂` such that
  `N₀ = ⟨h₁,h₂⟩` is again suitable"*.

## The shape of `thm:hull`

The conclusion of Hull's theorem asserts the existence of a group, so it is
carried by a structure — `HullQuotient` — whose fields are the printed clauses
one for one, and the cited statement is the `Nonempty` of that structure.  The
group structure on the target is an instance-implicit field, following
`Sofic/TorsionFreeFullMFRadical.lean`'s `DefectRoutingData`, so that the
remaining fields can be stated about it.
-/

namespace GroupApproximation
namespace Manuscript
namespace NonMF
namespace TorsionFree

open GroupApproximation.HullGeometry

universe u

/-! ## The conclusion of Hull's theorem -/

/-- **The output of `thm:hull`.**  Its fields are the printed clauses of Hull's
small cancellation theorem, one for one:

> Then there is a surjective homomorphism `q : G → Q` such that `Q` is
> acylindrically hyperbolic, `q|_F` is injective, `q(tᵢ) ∈ q(N)` for all `i`,
> and every element of finite order in `Q` is the image of an element of the
> same order in `G`.

The hypotheses of the theorem — that `G` is acylindrically hyperbolic and that
`N` is suitable with respect to `A` — are hypotheses of
`HullInputs.smallCancellation`, not fields here, so this structure records
precisely the conclusion. -/
structure HullQuotient {G : Type u} [Group G] (N : Subgroup G) {m : ℕ}
    (t : Fin m → G) (F : Set G) where
  /-- The target group `Q`. -/
  Q : Type u
  /-- Its group structure. -/
  [group : Group Q]
  /-- The homomorphism `q : G → Q`. -/
  q : G →* Q
  /-- `q` is surjective. -/
  surjective : Function.Surjective q
  /-- `Q` is acylindrically hyperbolic. -/
  [acylindricallyHyperbolic : IsAcylindricallyHyperbolic Q]
  /-- `q|_F` is injective. -/
  injOn : Set.InjOn q F
  /-- `q(tᵢ) ∈ q(N)` for every `i`. -/
  mem_map : ∀ i : Fin m, q (t i) ∈ N.map q
  /-- Every element of finite order in `Q` is the image of an element of the
  same order in `G`. -/
  finiteOrder_lift :
    ∀ y : Q, IsOfFinOrder y → ∃ g : G, q g = y ∧ orderOf g = orderOf y

namespace HullQuotient

variable {G : Type u} [Group G] {N : Subgroup G} {m : ℕ} {t : Fin m → G}
  {F : Set G}

instance instGroup (H : HullQuotient N t F) : Group H.Q := H.group

instance instIsAcylindricallyHyperbolic (H : HullQuotient N t F) :
    IsAcylindricallyHyperbolic H.Q :=
  H.acylindricallyHyperbolic

end HullQuotient

/-! ## The four citations, bundled as one hypothesis -/

/-- **The group-theoretic literature the manuscript cites but does not prove**,
in the section *"A torsion-free group with full MF radical"*.

A term of this structure is exactly the assertion that the four cited results
hold; it is never constructed here.  Consumers take it as a hypothesis, so the
citations travel with every statement that depends on them.

The sources are M. Hull, *Small cancellation in acylindrically hyperbolic
groups* (Theorem 7.1, Corollary 5.7, Lemma 5.8), and D. Osin, *Acylindrically
hyperbolic groups* (Lemma 7.1). -/
structure HullInputs : Type where
  /-- **`thm:hull`, Hull's small cancellation theorem** — Hull, Theorem 7.1.

  > Let `G` be acylindrically hyperbolic, let `N ≤ G` be suitable with respect
  > to `A`, and let `t₁, …, t_m ∈ G` and a finite subset `F ⊆ G` be given.  Then
  > there is a surjective homomorphism `q : G → Q` such that `Q` is
  > acylindrically hyperbolic, `q|_F` is injective, `q(tᵢ) ∈ q(N)` for all `i`,
  > and every element of finite order in `Q` is the image of an element of the
  > same order in `G`.

  This is the finite-set formulation the manuscript records; Hull's published
  theorem is the injectivity-radius formulation, and the reduction is the
  manuscript's preamble sentence about choosing a ball of `Γ(G,A)` containing
  `F`. -/
  smallCancellation : ∀ {G : Type u} [Group G] [IsAcylindricallyHyperbolic G]
    (A : HullGeneratingSet G) {N : Subgroup G}, Suitable A.alphabet N →
      ∀ {m : ℕ} (t : Fin m → G) {F : Set G}, F.Finite →
        Nonempty (HullQuotient N t F)
  /-- **The manuscript's separate remark on Hull's construction** — Hull,
  Theorem 7.1, by inspection of its construction.

  > Separately, inspection of Hull's construction, applied successively to
  > `t₁, …, t_m`, shows that `ker q` is normally generated by `m` elements.

  This is a property of Hull's construction rather than a consequence of the
  statement of `thm:hull`, so it is a citation of its own.  It is the sole
  reason the saturation quotient of `lem:saturation` is finitely presented. -/
  kerNormallyGenerated : ∀ {G : Type u} [Group G] {N : Subgroup G} {m : ℕ}
    {t : Fin m → G} {F : Set G} (H : HullQuotient N t F),
      ∃ S : Finset G, S.card ≤ m ∧ H.q.ker = Subgroup.normalClosure (S : Set G)
  /-- **Osin, Lemma 7.1**, in the form the proof of `lem:saturation` uses it:

  > Thus `N` acts non-elementarily on `Γ(G,A)` by Osin [Lemma 7.1].

  The hypothesis is that `N ∩ gNg⁻¹` is infinite for every `g ∈ G`, which is
  Osin's `s`-normality; the repository already carries it as
  `HullSuitable.IsSNormal`, written with the conjugation on the element rather
  than on the subgroup — the same condition, since `g` ranges over all of `G`.
  `Sofic/HullSuitableDefectSubgroup.lean` proves the group-theoretic half of the
  argument and records in its docstring that this geometric half is exactly
  Osin's Lemma 7.1, which is stated here for the first time. -/
  osinNonElementary : ∀ {G : Type u} [Group G] (A : HullGeneratingSet G)
    (N : Subgroup G), HullSuitable.IsSNormal N →
      ActsNonElementarily N (Cayley.base A.alphabet)
  /-- **Hull, Corollary 5.7 and Lemma 5.8**, in the form the proof of
  `lem:saturation` uses them:

  > By Hull [Corollary 5.7 and Lemma 5.8], `N` contains two elements `h₁, h₂`
  > such that `N₀ = ⟨h₁, h₂⟩` is again suitable.

  `Sofic/HullSuitableDefectSubgroup.lean` proves the non-geometric hypotheses of
  Hull's Lemma 5.8 — an element of infinite order generating a proper cyclic
  subgroup — over a torsion-free ambient group; what is cited here is the
  conclusion of the lemma, whose content is the hyperbolic embedding of
  `⟨h⟩`. -/
  twoGeneratedSuitable : ∀ {G : Type u} [Group G] (A : HullGeneratingSet G)
    {N : Subgroup G}, Suitable A.alphabet N →
      ∃ h₁ ∈ N, ∃ h₂ ∈ N,
        Suitable A.alphabet (Subgroup.closure ({h₁, h₂} : Set G))

end TorsionFree
end NonMF
end Manuscript
end GroupApproximation
